WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.390
[MUSICAL CHORD]

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.645
Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.645 --> 00:00:09.770
Thanks for having me today.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.870
It's a real honor to be
able to speak to you all.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:14.190
My name is John Burn-Murdoch.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.160
I'm going to be talking for
the next 40 minutes or so about

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.970
the lessons that I've learned
from visualizing the COVID-19

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.590
pandemic over--

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:26.530
well, I guess it's
now about 12 months.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.500
So to set the scene,
what I want to do

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.080
is start off by talking
about the perspective I'm

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.960
coming from in this talk.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.160
So data visualization
is obviously

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.280
an enormously broad church.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.380
There's all sorts of
different uses for dataviz

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.005
in different settings.

00:00:42.005 --> 00:00:43.380
But what I'm
talking about today,

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.270
specifically, is
data visualization

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.040
for a mass audience.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.190
Now, as a visual
journalist, I'm always

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.453
dealing with a large
audience, to some extent.

00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:55.120
The Financial Times
is behind a paywall,

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.140
so it's, perhaps, not the
largest audience of all.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.077
But what's really
changed dramatically

00:01:01.077 --> 00:01:02.910
over the last year,
working on the pandemic,

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.500
is that that audience
has become enormous.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.207
It's become truly global.

00:01:07.207 --> 00:01:08.790
And it was quite
obvious from as early

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:14.730
as the first couple of
weeks of March 2020 that I

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.130
was suddenly-- my
charts were suddenly

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.440
being seen by an
audience of millions

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.800
across the world instead of
just the tens of thousands

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.020
or hundreds of thousands
who would typically see them

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.630
on the Financial Times website.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.580
So the principles I'm going
to be talking about today,

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.820
and the best practices,
are all geared

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.230
towards communicating to
a hugely diverse audience

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.740
of people in terms of their
familiarity with charts.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.790
I'm not saying that
what I recommend today

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.620
is going to be the case
if you're producing

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.540
a visual installation
for an art gallery

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.690
or a coffee-table-style
piece of work.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.300
But really just thinking
about data visualization

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.030
fundamentally is an
act of communication,

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:58.120
and particularly of
mass communication.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:01.890
So to start with something that
I suspect reads as incredibly

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:06.539
obvious but I think is actually
relatively little discussed

00:02:06.539 --> 00:02:08.789
in the dataviz world, I'm
going to talk about the fact

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:11.340
that, to create really
effective, really

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.190
powerful charts, you
really need to understand

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:17.440
how people consume charts.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.750
Now, to talk a
little bit about why

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.010
that's not as obvious
as you might think,

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:25.030
if you look online at the
numbers of tutorials there are

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:28.420
for making graphics, making
dataviz, whether it's in R,

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:29.590
Python, D3--

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:33.010
you name it-- generally,
they're focused on the technical

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.950
element of
constructing a chart--

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.480
what chart type to choose,
how to get your geometries,

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.200
your shape, space, and color
exactly right, how to do things

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.260
more efficiently,
optimizing interactivity--

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.770
that kind of thing.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.910
There's relatively
little on thinking

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:56.050
about what more
qualitative elements

00:02:56.050 --> 00:03:01.060
you can optimize to ensure
that people understand,

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:04.390
enjoy, and remember your chart.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:08.200
And this is where I,
perhaps, get almost a bit

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.780
too corporate or
business-focused

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:11.590
in my view of this.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:15.200
But when you're making
charts for a mass audience,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.200
it serves you well, I
think, to think about them

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.350
almost as if you're
creating an advert.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.870
There is decades of
research from people

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:26.770
who create billboard ads, for
example, on what you should

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:31.150
do in terms of messaging, in
terms of how you use text,

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.790
in terms of how you use
color and all of that

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:38.000
to elicit a specific
response in your audience.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.630
And I think it's
that communication

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:42.520
way of thinking
that is relatively

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.370
underrepresented at the moment
in best-practice guidelines

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:46.330
for charts.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:49.030
So as I say, when I've
been creating charts

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.760
that I'm hoping and
latterly expecting

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.460
are going to be seen by
an audience of millions,

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.210
I've really had to
focus on, first of all,

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.300
how do I ensure
they're clear enough

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.880
to reach and be
easily understood

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.320
by millions of people?

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.380
And then how do I make sure
that they stick in their memory,

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:10.480
and they're not just
post 79 out of 100 that

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.150
ping on their Twitter
feed every day?

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:17.620
So without any further ado-- and
I think quite fittingly, given

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.779
that I'm talking about lessons
I learned from the pandemic--

00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:22.725
it's time to look
at the science.

00:04:22.725 --> 00:04:24.850
So I'm going to show you
here some work that is not

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:29.560
my own work, but it's probably
the single-most-influential

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.950
dataviz research paper
that I've ever come across.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:36.400
And it's one that I and
our wider team at the FT use

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.155
very frequently
and constantly have

00:04:38.155 --> 00:04:40.180
in our mind when we're
thinking about how

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.820
to optimize our work
for understanding

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:44.170
by a mass audience.

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:46.240
And that is this
paper from a team

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.710
led by Michelle Borkin
in the US on what

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:52.270
it is-- what are
the characteristics

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.130
of visualizations
that lead people

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.890
to recognize the
story of that graphic

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:59.000
and then to recall it later on?

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.660
So the slide I'm going
to show you in a second

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:06.448
is looking at an eye-tracking
experiment from this paper.

00:05:06.448 --> 00:05:08.740
And I'm going to show you a
GIF, so it's going to loop.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:10.573
If you don't see it
first time, that's fine.

00:05:10.573 --> 00:05:11.410
It'll come again.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.680
But what it is is it's showing
where people's eyes actually

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.680
focus when they're given
a chart for a relatively

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:18.820
short period of time--

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.930
10 seconds, 30 seconds or so--

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.470
and asked, what do you see here?

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:24.460
What can you make out?

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.170
What's the main takeaway?

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:30.640
And so what you're going to
see is a simple illustration

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.040
of a chart and then a blue
dot on that chart showing

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:35.650
where people look when they're
presented with that chart.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:41.120
And what you see
is, that blue dot

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.853
moves straight up to
the title of the chart,

00:05:43.853 --> 00:05:45.270
has a little explore
of the title,

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.330
then comes down and
reads off the axis label

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:49.880
to see what units
the chart is showing,

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:52.190
and then reads across
the chart content itself.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:54.050
I'll just let that play again.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:55.950
So the eyes go to the title.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.250
They then come down to
see the vertical axis.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:02.030
And then they read across
the contents of the chart.

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:05.600
The key point here is that that
initial focus goes straight

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.330
to the title.

00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:10.780
And the researchers did
additional follow-up analysis

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.880
of the same group of
participants and found that

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.800
when people, later on,
were asked to remember any

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.290
information about the chart
and just write a free-form

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:22.470
description of what they'd
seen, words featured very

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:27.210
prominently-- the title,
any annotations, labeling--

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:29.430
were all mentioned
far more often

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:32.370
when people remembered the chart
than the actual visual content

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.560
of the plot itself.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.860
And that is something
we really center

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.110
on in our work at
the FT. Our view

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:45.390
is very much that you can
make a stunning chart.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.370
You can have all
your geometry right.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:49.660
It can be technically
executed perfectly.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.330
But if the messaging--
if the text--

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.480
is absent, in the
worst-case scenario,

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.450
or too sterile, too
dry, you're going

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.490
to lose a hell of
a lot of people.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:01.530
And the way I like
to think about that

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:06.810
is that your text can help
your chart be understandable,

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:08.910
enjoyable, and memorable
for someone who is,

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:13.170
quote unquote, "not
a chart person."

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.570
Those of us watching
or participating

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:17.340
in the conference
today, I'm sure,

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.100
consider ourselves chart
people, or data people,

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:21.460
to a greater or lesser extent.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:23.520
And that means,
when we see a chart,

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.430
it's already a thing that we're
familiar with and that we like,

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:29.427
and that we have a sort
of way of understanding,

00:07:29.427 --> 00:07:32.010
whereas there are millions and
millions and millions of people

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:32.700
out there--

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:36.130
a lot more non-chart people
than chart people, as it were--

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:38.610
who need that helping hand.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:40.898
So to give you an
example from the pandemic

00:07:40.898 --> 00:07:42.690
of this sort of way of
thinking and action,

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:46.060
I'm going to talk through
the origin story, as it were,

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.870
of our coronavirus
trajectory tracker--

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:50.790
so probably the
most high-profile

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:54.550
chart that we've put out
over the last 12 months.

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:57.622
And I'm going to start by
showing how this was sparked--

00:07:57.622 --> 00:07:59.580
how this came about-- by
showing you an email I

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.890
got from one of my colleagues.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:05.440
I think this was March
the 10th or 11th.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:07.650
And so she's one of my
general news reporters

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:12.270
who was covering this
nascent pandemic back then,

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:13.830
or certainly the
nascent epidemic,

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:16.230
as far as the UK and
Europe was concerned.

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.390
And she just asked
me-- she's wondering

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:21.570
if I had any data on daily
cases in Italy, which,

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:24.640
of course, at the time, was
the focus of all the world's

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:25.140
attention.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.973
There were grim pictures
coming out of there

00:08:26.973 --> 00:08:31.737
in the news reports of bodies in
morgues and that kind of thing.

00:08:31.737 --> 00:08:33.570
And she's asking me,
OK, do we have any data

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:34.740
on daily cases for Italy?

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:37.200
And can we compare that to the
UK, perhaps other countries

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.070
as well?

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:40.929
She hadn't managed to find
that data anywhere else.

00:08:40.929 --> 00:08:44.560
And so I rustled up, that
same afternoon, or evening,

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.210
or whatever it was,
a quick version--

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.920
version naught point
O, as it were--

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:54.720
of what became our high-profile
trajectory trackers.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.220
So you can see, for
those of you who

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.475
remember the final
version of this chart,

00:08:58.475 --> 00:08:59.850
or they've
encountered by the way

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.490
of this chart, the geometry--

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.930
what is shown, what
is plotted here--

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:06.930
is essentially unchanged
from the final version.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:09.150
We've got the number of
days since the country first

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:12.540
reached its 100th confirmed
case going horizontally.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.490
And then you've
got a log scale--

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:17.610
a logarithmic y-axis--
of the total number--

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:21.030
cumulative number-- of confirmed
cases up to that point,

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:23.370
and a set of countries
shown on there

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:27.240
colored by things like their
global region or other aspects

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:28.990
that I'll come onto in a minute.

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:31.920
But obviously, if
I published that, I

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.030
doubt it would have cut through
to a huge audience the way

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:35.790
it ultimately did.

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.210
There's nothing here, really,
telling you what's going on.

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:40.350
There's a lot of
assumed knowledge.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:42.012
Obviously, I sent
that to my colleague

00:09:42.012 --> 00:09:43.470
in the context of
this email chain.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.570
We both knew what we were
talking about and looking at.

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:49.650
But if I just put this-- if
we published this on the FT,

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:52.560
on social media, a
few people-- again,

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:55.090
chart people or
COVID-data people--

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:56.340
would have thought, oh, right.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:57.430
Interesting.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:01.600
But it wouldn't have
had any mass resonance.

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:04.800
So here's the next
version of this

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:08.550
when it's been styled, as
we style all our charts

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:09.570
on the Financial Times.

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:14.760
So we've got that trademark
salmon-pink background.

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.460
I'm now using the colors
from our main color palette.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:20.290
Again, doing some sort of
semantic use of color there.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:24.452
So I'm highlighting some Asian
countries in this light blue.

00:10:24.452 --> 00:10:26.160
I've got South Korea
in a different blue,

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:27.910
because it wasn't clear
what trajectory it

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:30.960
was on by that point, and a
few other colors dotted around.

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:33.030
There's a bit more
text on the chart now.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:35.910
We've got both axes labeled.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:38.740
I've got that source and
footnote at the bottom as well.

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:43.110
But again, sure, in terms of
aesthetics, in terms of design,

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:44.970
this is now neater.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:48.210
This is a better fit for what
we would publish on the FT.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:51.112
But it's still missing
those crucial ingredients.

00:10:51.112 --> 00:10:53.070
So here's the final
version that was published.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.890
I think this was
on March the 12th--

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:58.090
so still about two
weeks before the UK,

00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:00.060
for example, actually
went into lockdown.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:02.442
And now we've got all of that
additional labeling added.

00:11:02.442 --> 00:11:03.900
Again, going back
to that research,

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.737
these are the key
bits of information

00:11:05.737 --> 00:11:08.320
that are going to be the first
thing that someone encountering

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:09.300
this chart focuses on.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.550
And they're going to set the
scene for everything else

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:13.210
that they take away from it.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:15.840
So we've got a title, a very
active title-- descriptive

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.940
narrative title-- that
most Western countries

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:19.980
are on the same
coronavirus trajectories.

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:21.645
Hong Kong and
Singapore, by contrast,

00:11:21.645 --> 00:11:23.610
have managed to slow the spread.

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:25.860
So straightaway, you've got
a message which grabs you,

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:27.943
which brings you in, and
which tells you something

00:11:27.943 --> 00:11:29.333
that is happening in the world.

00:11:29.333 --> 00:11:30.750
The fact that
there's a chart here

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:32.760
as well is almost incidental.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:34.560
Maybe I'm exaggerating there.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:36.770
But the point is,
whether or not you

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:40.520
are someone who builds and works
with dataviz multiple times

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:44.570
a day or someone who last really
spent any time with charts when

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:48.320
you were age 16 and doing some
compulsory maths or science

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.260
education, whichever of
those groups you fall into,

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:53.408
the words here tell
you what's going on.

00:11:53.408 --> 00:11:54.950
And so if you are
someone who doesn't

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:56.840
consider themselves
a chart person,

00:11:56.840 --> 00:11:58.070
you've got your entry point.

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:00.500
You can now understand
the geometry of the chart.

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:03.020
You can work out what's
going on with the lines,

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:05.540
with the shape, with the space,
with the color because you

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:07.940
know what message
it's trying to tell.

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:10.400
If the chart simply
said "cumulative number

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:14.120
of cases by number of days
since 100th case" as the title,

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:15.947
that's a hell of a
lot more technical.

00:12:15.947 --> 00:12:17.030
There's a lot more jargon.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:20.120
And it's going to be much
less accessible to people who

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:21.710
are not familiar with charts.

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:23.390
The other thing we've
obviously got here

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:25.765
is these annotations towards
the right-hand side actually

00:12:25.765 --> 00:12:28.530
explaining some of the stuff
you can see on the chart.

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.200
So this is more
geared towards someone

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:35.480
who has not been following
the pandemic and COVID data

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:37.040
in a huge amount of detail.

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.560
They immediately
have the queries

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:41.690
that they would have had
of this chart answered.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:43.090
So they can see, oh, right.

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:47.120
So Japan-- its line
is further down.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:50.357
The virus is spreading less
fast there and less widely.

00:12:50.357 --> 00:12:52.190
But we can see that
it's not quite clear yet

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:54.190
whether the spread has
actually slowed in Japan,

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:55.910
or they're not doing
enough testing.

00:12:55.910 --> 00:12:58.910
Korea, we can see, which
was, of course, the big focus

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:01.610
here as we were looking for
how countries could get off

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:03.140
that steep curve and flatten.

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:05.430
We've got an explanation
there about how

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:07.460
it has slowed its spread
from the initial pace.

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:09.460
And you can also look at
Singapore and Hong Kong

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:10.910
and see what they've done right.

00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:14.420
So that text, when we looked
at what people were talking

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:17.480
about around the context of
this graphic on social media

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:19.400
and on the Financial
Times website--

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:21.440
that text was the
key part that really

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:23.900
seemed to cut through and cause
people to keep coming back

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:25.580
to this chart and,
indeed, to share

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:27.840
this chart with other people.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.890
So the first lesson of the
pandemic, for me, I think,

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:34.610
was that using text and
other annotation is really

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:37.340
critical for making
sure a chart goes

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:39.410
from being a chart
for dataviz people,

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:42.320
shall we say, to a
chart that is truly

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:46.430
accessible to a really
mass audience of people

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:48.830
who haven't been--

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:51.390
they're not involved in
charts and data every day.

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:54.290
And suddenly, this is
the democratization

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:58.400
of charts by using text to
make them more accessible.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:00.280
The next lesson--
and this is, for me,

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.500
one of the most fundamental
lessons anyone can learn

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:05.290
in the process of
building dataviz--

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:09.040
is that there's a big difference
between making the smartest

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:10.970
version of a chart
you possibly can

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:13.475
and the clearest version of
a chart you possibly can.

00:14:13.475 --> 00:14:15.100
As with everything,
there are obviously

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:17.150
exceptions where you can
do both of these things.

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:18.733
But during the
pandemic, I think there

00:14:18.733 --> 00:14:21.910
have been several examples
of how these really do--

00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:26.880
these really are in tension with
one another a lot of the time.

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:28.790
So sometimes-- and
this was more the case,

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:31.910
I think, in the earlier
part of my career.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:33.967
But it's still a
recurring theme.

00:14:33.967 --> 00:14:36.050
And I think this is something
that a lot of people

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:37.802
here today will
recognize as well.

00:14:37.802 --> 00:14:39.260
Sometimes we can
get into the habit

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:42.860
of thinking that effective
data visualization is

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.980
about optimizing for
precision and objectivity.

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:50.370
There's a list of
technical tasks.

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:52.010
And if you nail
every one of them,

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:53.840
you'll make the perfect chart.

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:57.270
You're using maths and geometry
to arrive at the best solution.

00:14:57.270 --> 00:15:03.020
It's a very technical,
structured process.

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:04.700
But then I actually
publish a chart,

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:07.370
and it suddenly becomes very
clear that that is simply

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:08.100
not the case.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:12.140
That is a convenient
but very rarely true

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:14.600
description of what we
should be aiming for when

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:17.790
we make a data visualization.

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:19.670
So I'm going to come
back to this same chart

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:23.360
and talk through all of
the clever technical things

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.300
that I thought I was doing
with this chart when I made it,

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.120
and then how the chart, then--

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:32.030
when the chart encountered
reality, shall we say,

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:35.510
a lot of my assumptions
were turned on their head.

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:39.020
So the first difference-- and
I'm just going to flip back

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:40.427
between these two charts--

00:15:40.427 --> 00:15:42.260
this is the one that
I've shown you already.

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:45.920
This was the data plotted
on a logarithmic y-axis.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:48.410
The point of this
was to say, well,

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:51.350
we know that the pandemic,
especially in the early stage,

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:53.370
spreads exponentially.

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:58.460
So the shape of the
curves on this chart--

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:01.760
if we plotted it on a linear
axis, on a linear scale,

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:03.350
they would all be
arching upwards,

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:05.420
from gentle slopes
to steep slopes.

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:07.760
And to me, you're using
a lot of visual bandwidth

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:10.633
there just to show something
that we assume is the default--

00:16:10.633 --> 00:16:12.050
that all of these
curves are going

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:14.870
to be getting steeper and
steeper in these early weeks.

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:17.870
So by going for a log
scale, you free up

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:20.282
more bandwidth, as it
were, in the reader's head.

00:16:20.282 --> 00:16:21.740
And you allow them
to focus on what

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:25.550
matters, which is, you can now
do the key thing that readers,

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:27.320
at this stage, wanted
to do, which is

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.810
compare the trajectories
of different countries

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:31.160
on a straight line.

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:33.710
You can look at this
and say, OK, the virus

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:35.540
seems to be spreading
more quickly in Spain

00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:39.680
than in France, and France, in
turn, more quickly than the UK.

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:41.750
You can also, because
it's a straight line,

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:44.510
forecast out ahead
and work out where

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:46.730
the rate of cases
in your country

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:49.970
is going to be a few days or
a week further down the line.

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:51.560
You can answer that
critical question

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:54.140
that we set out to answer
here, if we refer back

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:56.660
to my colleague's email,
which is how our country is

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:57.830
doing compared to Italy.

00:16:57.830 --> 00:16:59.330
You can answer that
much more easily

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:00.800
with these straight
lines that are

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:04.088
easy to extrapolate than with
the curves, where it's harder

00:17:04.088 --> 00:17:05.630
to work out exactly
where a line will

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:08.319
be at a certain point in time.

00:17:08.319 --> 00:17:10.890
So my thinking here was
all framed around this idea

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:14.490
that, when someone is looking at
this chart, the whole framework

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:16.800
they're operating in
that determines what

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:19.020
their takeaway
message is, is about,

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:22.050
are these two countries or three
countries on the same course?

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:24.945
Or, how many days until
the country average,

00:17:24.945 --> 00:17:27.750
then, is at a certain
level of cases

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:30.510
that I know means something?

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:34.140
And therefore, that they're
not thinking, well, how many

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:35.820
pixels represent 100 cases?

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:38.850
They're not worried about
what's going on in the y-axis.

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:42.190
They're looking for more
of an overarching message.

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:45.487
However, let's have a look
at some of the feedback that

00:17:45.487 --> 00:17:46.570
started pouring into that.

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:49.120
And I will say, just
as a quick aside here,

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:51.637
for anyone who isn't in
the habit of publishing

00:17:51.637 --> 00:17:54.220
their work on social media-- and
I know it's not for everyone,

00:17:54.220 --> 00:17:57.820
and there are certainly some
suboptimal features of Twitter,

00:17:57.820 --> 00:17:58.870
shall we say.

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:01.090
But it's an incredible
way to work out

00:18:01.090 --> 00:18:03.250
what does and doesn't
work in a chart

00:18:03.250 --> 00:18:07.000
and to gauge the reaction,
again, of both chart

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:11.120
people and non-chart
people very quickly.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:15.640
So I was inundated with
messages around responding

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:18.490
to these charts for week
after week after week,

00:18:18.490 --> 00:18:20.380
and continue to be this year.

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:23.170
But this was a snapshot of
some of the initial feedback

00:18:23.170 --> 00:18:23.830
to that chart.

00:18:23.830 --> 00:18:28.750
Now, obviously, loads of people
understood the log axis fine.

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:32.230
But any critical
feedback is always worth

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:33.290
paying attention to.

00:18:33.290 --> 00:18:36.670
And the fact that the majority
of people got it doesn't negate

00:18:36.670 --> 00:18:38.420
the fact that plenty
of people didn't.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:41.080
So lots of people here
very confused by what's

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:42.070
going on in the y-axis.

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:44.800
Some of them, perhaps, know what
a log scale is but just thought

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:46.210
it wasn't clearly explained.

00:18:46.210 --> 00:18:49.330
Others clearly are just baffled.

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:52.240
And that's not a reaction
you want with your chart.

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:57.490
However, my response to that was
not to just think, oh, right.

00:18:57.490 --> 00:18:58.960
OK, back to the drawing board.

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:00.640
Start again.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:01.630
It's got to be linear.

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:02.755
People aren't getting this.

00:19:02.755 --> 00:19:05.650
My response was to
think, OK, I've got a--

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:07.720
I'm in an advantageous
position here,

00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:11.410
that I'm on Twitter where
I'm presenting these charts.

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:15.050
And this is a space where I can
continue these conversations.

00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:20.060
So what I did was I immediately
posted a long explanation--

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:23.110
well, not too long, but a
relatively thorough explanation

00:19:23.110 --> 00:19:26.230
of exactly why
logarithmic y-axes

00:19:26.230 --> 00:19:30.640
were well suited to visualizing
data that grows exponentially.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:32.770
That got a very good response.

00:19:32.770 --> 00:19:35.620
And we followed up
on that, at the FT,

00:19:35.620 --> 00:19:37.750
by actually making a whole
video explainer which

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:39.160
talked about all
of the decisions

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:40.827
we were making when
we made these charts

00:19:40.827 --> 00:19:42.460
and how to understand them.

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:45.040
And this seemed to work
really, really well

00:19:45.040 --> 00:19:49.953
in terms of softening some
of that initial confusion

00:19:49.953 --> 00:19:51.370
that some people
had to the chart.

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:53.650
And my favorite
thing about this is

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:56.980
that, what you get when you do
this kind of work, what you'll

00:19:56.980 --> 00:19:59.890
see when you explain
things clearly

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:01.390
and have them in
the public domain,

00:20:01.390 --> 00:20:07.000
whether it's Twitter, Facebook,
whatever, is your audience

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:11.057
will start helping you explain
this stuff to other people.

00:20:11.057 --> 00:20:12.640
So what we started
to see, quite soon,

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:15.370
was these other people
jumping into the replies

00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:18.937
to me, explaining to the
people who had been confused,

00:20:18.937 --> 00:20:20.020
look, this is a log scale.

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:21.310
This is what they do.

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:23.320
Don't worry about it.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:25.630
And I'm actually pointing
people to the video

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:26.960
where I explain this.

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:32.680
So instead of thinking, well,
I just published the chart.

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:33.310
I'm done.

00:20:33.310 --> 00:20:34.810
I'm going to walk away--

00:20:34.810 --> 00:20:38.890
by staying in that
conversation around the chart,

00:20:38.890 --> 00:20:42.880
I was able to ensure that
as few people as possible

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:45.580
actually remained
confused and to really

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:48.070
try to improve understanding
of log scales full stop.

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:51.220
And just as a quick aside,
something I find quite amusing

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:53.470
when I look back at these
messages is that you'll see,

00:20:53.470 --> 00:20:56.860
on the left-hand side there,
two of the people who had been

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:58.870
confused by the log scale--

00:20:58.870 --> 00:20:59.860
well, one of them--

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:01.210
their account is now suspended.

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:03.335
And the other one-- their
account no longer exists.

00:21:03.335 --> 00:21:05.650
So I like to think of that
as being a little example

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:09.070
that people who don't
understand log scales

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:11.020
have other failings
in life, shall we say,

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:12.850
that lead them to be
kicked off Twitter.

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:15.970
Anyway, back to the
important stuff.

00:21:15.970 --> 00:21:18.270
All of this about log
scales and people,

00:21:18.270 --> 00:21:20.020
perhaps, initially,
not understanding them

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:21.895
but gradually getting
their heads around them

00:21:21.895 --> 00:21:25.120
brings me onto what, in
a huge sea of contenders,

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:27.100
is actually my favorite
chart of the pandemic.

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:29.800
And that is this.

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:33.430
What you're looking at here is
data from Google Trends, which,

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:35.890
for the unfamiliar,
is measuring how

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:38.767
much search interest on Google
there is for topics over time.

00:21:38.767 --> 00:21:40.600
And what we see here
is, this is the numbers

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:46.330
of people, worldwide, searching
for "log scale" on Google.

00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:49.690
And I've got that going back for
the last five years or so-- so

00:21:49.690 --> 00:22:00.340
from late 2015
through to mid-2020.

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:02.470
And what you see
there is a big spike.

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:04.900
Searches for "log scale"
in that five-year period

00:22:04.900 --> 00:22:08.860
reach their peak on around
the 22nd of March, 2020.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:11.140
And coincidentally,
that was about a week

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:13.810
after we started
publishing these log scale

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:16.652
charts on the FT.
And the interest--

00:22:16.652 --> 00:22:18.610
the numbers of people
looking at those charts--

00:22:18.610 --> 00:22:20.120
had really ramped
up over that week.

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:24.060
So I don't think it's
unreasonable for me, and for us

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:27.430
at the FT, to think
that the proliferation

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:31.600
of log-scale charts and our
use of explainers around them

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:34.660
really did help actually educate
a hell of a lot of people

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:35.830
on what log scales are.

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:38.650
And I know that from other
anecdotal evidence as well.

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:41.000
I had school friends
who I'd not spoken to

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:42.500
for 15 years getting
in touch saying

00:22:42.500 --> 00:22:44.390
they were explaining
my charts to their mom

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:45.348
and that kind of thing.

00:22:45.348 --> 00:22:49.237
So it was a real demonstration
that, by sticking around

00:22:49.237 --> 00:22:51.320
and actually explaining
what your chart is showing

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:54.530
to people, you can
really help people

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:57.510
become more visually literate.

00:22:57.510 --> 00:23:00.450
So the lesson there--
yeah-- is that, if there

00:23:00.450 --> 00:23:04.092
is confusion around a chart
that you've made, that's on you.

00:23:04.092 --> 00:23:06.300
It's no good just saying,
oh, how do these people not

00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:07.440
understand log scales?

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:11.580
You've got to do the work to
make sure that the people--

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:14.080
you've got to do the work to
make sure that your audience is

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:15.683
not left confused.

00:23:15.683 --> 00:23:17.350
And the key point
here, for me, as well,

00:23:17.350 --> 00:23:20.200
is doing this additional
explanation in situ--

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:22.690
doing this explanation
where the charts appeared.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:26.200
It's not much use publishing
a chart over here and then,

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:29.440
a few days later, explaining how
it was meant to work over here.

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:32.883
You've got to try and do
that where your audience is

00:23:32.883 --> 00:23:35.050
so that they are not going
to get lost along the way

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:38.730
and walk away
scratching their head.

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:40.710
But there's more.

00:23:40.710 --> 00:23:42.750
The reaction to
these log scales were

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:47.250
not purely about the log
scales, or about the geometry,

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:49.098
about the space,
about the numbers.

00:23:49.098 --> 00:23:51.640
When you look at some of these
other responses, a lot of them

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:54.070
were much more qualitative.

00:23:54.070 --> 00:23:55.360
Or, they were quite personal.

00:23:55.360 --> 00:23:58.000
In some cases, they
were even political.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:01.450
So I'm sure I don't
need to explain

00:24:01.450 --> 00:24:03.670
to everyone that the
pandemic, COVID has all

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:09.080
become quite politicized, quite
partisan, over the last year.

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:12.130
There are camps of
pro-maskers and anti-maskers,

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:14.710
pro-lockdown, anti-lockdown--
all of this stuff.

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:17.680
And it turns out that the
use of log scales in charts

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:19.880
is actually another
example of this.

00:24:19.880 --> 00:24:21.910
So we had some people
saying that they

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:28.360
felt like the use of a log
scale was dampening the impact

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:30.830
of increased numbers of cases.

00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:32.830
So a line went up and up and up.

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:38.290
It then started to gradually
flatten as the rate of increase

00:24:38.290 --> 00:24:40.570
slowed, even if the numbers
were still going up.

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:45.550
So people felt like
we were diminishing--

00:24:45.550 --> 00:24:48.650
we were downplaying the
impact of increased spread

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:51.993
as time went on, because those
lines were becoming flatter,

00:24:51.993 --> 00:24:53.660
whereas if they were
linear, they'd have

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:55.855
been going straight upwards.

00:24:55.855 --> 00:24:58.010
And yeah, people really
felt that we were, perhaps,

00:24:58.010 --> 00:25:00.010
trying to make some kind
of political point here

00:25:00.010 --> 00:25:02.000
and trying to suggest
that, in the UK,

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:05.350
for example, things weren't
as bad as they were.

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:08.140
So the clear takeaway
from that seems

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:12.040
to be that, quite apart
from people reacting

00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:14.680
to the actual data and
geometry in your charts,

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:17.110
they're going to come in
with all sorts of baggage,

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:20.770
as it were, in terms of their
understandings and feelings

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:21.850
about the subject matter.

00:25:21.850 --> 00:25:23.308
And there may be
decisions that you

00:25:23.308 --> 00:25:25.030
made in the construction
of your charts

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:27.670
that you felt were
purely technical,

00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:30.023
purely to do with dataviz.

00:25:30.023 --> 00:25:32.440
But people are actually going
to read into that a lot more

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:34.260
than what you intended.

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:36.760
So generally, there seemed to be
two camps of people, roughly,

00:25:36.760 --> 00:25:38.052
who would look at these charts.

00:25:38.052 --> 00:25:41.913
There were people who, like
us, in this session today,

00:25:41.913 --> 00:25:44.330
who would just look at these
numbers and think, all right.

00:25:44.470 --> 00:25:45.810
Yeah, what's going on there?

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:50.200
And a very analytical way
of analyzing the chart.

00:25:50.200 --> 00:25:53.170
But there was a huge number of
others who-- and I think this

00:25:53.170 --> 00:25:54.670
goes back to my
first point, really,

00:25:54.670 --> 00:25:56.253
as well, about the
use of text and how

00:25:56.253 --> 00:25:57.430
people understand charts.

00:25:57.430 --> 00:25:59.680
They would just take away a
single overarching message

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:00.730
from these charts.

00:26:00.730 --> 00:26:02.320
And so they would
look at something

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:05.050
where the lines were
becoming a bit more gentle,

00:26:05.050 --> 00:26:06.610
and they would
think, oh, this chart

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:09.045
is trying to say things aren't
actually that bad at all.

00:26:09.045 --> 00:26:11.170
And so it's just a really
interesting demonstration

00:26:11.170 --> 00:26:13.450
of how you need to
cater to those needs.

00:26:13.450 --> 00:26:16.030
You don't want someone to
have a negative reaction

00:26:16.030 --> 00:26:19.840
to your chart based on some
design choice that you made,

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:24.350
even if you hadn't realized what
impact it was going to have.

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:27.983
So yeah, this can lead people
to completely throw out

00:26:27.983 --> 00:26:29.150
the baby with the bathwater.

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:31.940
They'll decide that
this chart is wrong,

00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:35.660
it's worthless, it's
junk, because it

00:26:35.660 --> 00:26:38.980
jars with their understanding
of the subject matter.

00:26:38.980 --> 00:26:41.050
So this is the
next lesson for us,

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:46.110
which was that information
visualization, dataviz, is

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:48.720
about much more than
just pixels on a page,

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:52.020
and geometry, and shape,
and space, and color.

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:54.480
It's about the whole feeling
that something creates

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:56.220
and whether that
meshes with or jars

00:26:56.220 --> 00:27:00.160
with someone's perceptions
of the topic at hand.

00:27:00.160 --> 00:27:03.650
So we need to think about that
when we're producing a chart,

00:27:03.650 --> 00:27:09.180
especially on a divisive or
sensitive topic like this,

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:11.613
and minimize the risk that
someone is going to react badly

00:27:11.613 --> 00:27:13.530
because of some choices
that we've consciously

00:27:13.530 --> 00:27:15.190
made in the geometry.

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:17.225
If someone's objecting
to the data itself,

00:27:17.225 --> 00:27:18.100
that's their problem.

00:27:18.100 --> 00:27:22.630
If someone was looking at
this chart and saying, oh,

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:24.880
why are you showing
COVID cases going up

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:26.500
when the pandemic is over?

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:28.180
Well, that would
be flat-out wrong

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:30.130
because COVID cases
were going up.

00:27:30.130 --> 00:27:33.070
But if someone is objecting to
the use of a different scale,

00:27:33.070 --> 00:27:34.960
for example, and
the tone, the feel

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:38.530
that that gives to your chart,
I think that's not unreasonable.

00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:40.370
Their feelings are what
their feelings are,

00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:42.328
and it's important for
us to consider that when

00:27:42.328 --> 00:27:45.370
we're designing our charts.

00:27:45.370 --> 00:27:47.080
So what we did in
the end of the FT

00:27:47.080 --> 00:27:49.930
was we turned these
static graphics

00:27:49.930 --> 00:27:52.780
that we'd been producing
every day with a log scale

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:54.610
into an interactive
piece where you can

00:27:54.610 --> 00:27:56.800
choose between log or linear.

00:27:56.800 --> 00:27:59.650
So anyone, then, complaining
about not understanding

00:27:59.650 --> 00:28:04.360
or being misled by the use
of a log scale, for example,

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:07.210
could then flit between the
two-- toggle between the two--

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:07.990
on our page here.

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:09.910
And that's what many,
many, many people

00:28:09.910 --> 00:28:12.910
have done over the
course of the last year.

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:14.410
The next point--
and this comes back

00:28:14.410 --> 00:28:17.860
to what I was saying earlier
about the value of something

00:28:17.860 --> 00:28:19.750
like social media or
whatever platform you're

00:28:19.750 --> 00:28:22.240
publishing your charts on.

00:28:22.240 --> 00:28:25.480
And I want to talk about how
you can gain an enormous amount

00:28:25.480 --> 00:28:28.270
from thinking about
dataviz, again,

00:28:28.270 --> 00:28:32.380
as a fundamental act of
communication, as a dialogue.

00:28:32.380 --> 00:28:35.095
So instead of just throwing
your chart out there

00:28:35.095 --> 00:28:37.180
and walking away,
with explosions

00:28:37.180 --> 00:28:40.060
in the background, as
it were, stick around,

00:28:40.060 --> 00:28:42.340
get into the conversation,
and see what you can learn

00:28:42.340 --> 00:28:45.610
and what you can gain from those
conversations about the work

00:28:45.610 --> 00:28:47.620
that you've done.

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:49.240
I just-- I think it's--

00:28:49.240 --> 00:28:52.270
I feel so fortunate,
as it were, to have

00:28:52.270 --> 00:28:55.270
had the number of interactions
I've had with people all

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:57.470
over the world, from
all walks of life,

00:28:57.470 --> 00:28:59.800
all levels of data
and visual literacy,

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:02.830
about the charts that I've
built, because I have,

00:29:02.830 --> 00:29:06.825
now, an enormous amount of
information on what people have

00:29:06.825 --> 00:29:08.200
liked, what people
haven't liked,

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:10.450
what people have struggled
with, what people have been

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:13.210
challenged by but risen
to, what people have just

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:15.248
thrown their hands up
at and not liked at all.

00:29:15.248 --> 00:29:17.290
And so getting into those
conversations, I think,

00:29:17.290 --> 00:29:18.873
is absolutely critical,
whether you're

00:29:18.873 --> 00:29:21.580
publishing this as a
journalist to a mass audience,

00:29:21.580 --> 00:29:24.850
whether it's to colleagues
at your workplace--

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:26.210
wherever it is.

00:29:26.210 --> 00:29:29.950
So to do that, one thing
we did very explicitly,

00:29:29.950 --> 00:29:32.890
from quite early on
in our visual coverage

00:29:32.890 --> 00:29:34.990
of the pandemic,
was we explicitly

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:37.603
solicited this kind of
feedback, because sure,

00:29:37.603 --> 00:29:39.520
as I've shown you with
some of those responses

00:29:39.520 --> 00:29:41.950
to the log scales,
the nature of Twitter

00:29:41.950 --> 00:29:45.340
is that you will get feedback
whether you ask for it or not.

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:47.890
But I think, as
anyone who's thought

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:49.750
about this for any
amount of time knows,

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:53.110
the quality of feedback you will
get goes up through the roof--

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:54.910
or exponentially,
one might say--

00:29:54.910 --> 00:29:57.800
when you actually ask for
that, because first of all,

00:29:57.800 --> 00:29:59.590
there are a hell of
a lot of people--

00:29:59.590 --> 00:30:03.280
the majority of people,
in fact, aren't just

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:09.130
going to randomly hit a
stranger with a load of thoughts

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:10.720
on the work that
they've just done.

00:30:10.720 --> 00:30:14.130
A lot of people are
polite, or they're shy.

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:16.240
This is just not how
they work online.

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:18.910
Whereas, when you actively
go out there and say,

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:20.890
let us know what you
think about the stuff

00:30:20.890 --> 00:30:21.700
that we've been doing here.

00:30:21.700 --> 00:30:22.450
What do you think
of these charts?

00:30:22.450 --> 00:30:22.992
Do they work?

00:30:22.992 --> 00:30:23.650
Do they not?

00:30:23.650 --> 00:30:24.917
Any other thoughts you have--

00:30:24.917 --> 00:30:26.500
First of all, you're
opening the door.

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:30.670
You're telling these people
who are maybe more introverted

00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:32.492
or just don't see
it as their place

00:30:32.492 --> 00:30:34.450
to comment-- you're
telling them that, yeah, we

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:37.120
want to hear your thoughts.

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:41.950
But you're also, I think,
making that-- any criticism

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:44.590
you receive much more
likely to be constructive,

00:30:44.590 --> 00:30:46.930
because people now know that
their criticism is going

00:30:46.930 --> 00:30:49.210
to be acted upon,
and they're not just

00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:51.010
shouting into the screen.

00:30:51.010 --> 00:30:54.940
So we would explicitly ask
for feedback over email,

00:30:54.940 --> 00:30:57.160
over Twitter direct
messages and the replies.

00:30:57.160 --> 00:31:00.220
We actually ended up setting
up a completely separate inbox

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:03.340
just for people to talk
to us about these charts.

00:31:03.340 --> 00:31:06.160
And this is just a snapshot
from one day in April.

00:31:06.160 --> 00:31:09.820
And this was a full
screen of Gmail.

00:31:09.820 --> 00:31:12.950
But this one day's messages
spanned more than one

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:13.450
full screen.

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:15.970
So I think we had
about 100 messages

00:31:15.970 --> 00:31:18.280
in one day about this chart.

00:31:18.280 --> 00:31:21.385
And I don't think people will
be able to read the text there.

00:31:21.385 --> 00:31:23.260
But if I just talk
through the range of stuff

00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:25.582
we were getting here,
we've got people

00:31:25.582 --> 00:31:27.790
pointing us to data sources
that we didn't know about

00:31:27.790 --> 00:31:29.440
before for countries
in the world

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:31.840
that we didn't previously
include in our charts.

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:34.037
We've got people
pointing out things

00:31:34.037 --> 00:31:35.620
that they struggled
with or would like

00:31:35.620 --> 00:31:37.270
us to change in our charts.

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:39.850
We've got people just thanking
us for doing these charts.

00:31:39.850 --> 00:31:42.400
We've got people who,
maybe a bit less politely,

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:44.290
are just saying, I
didn't like this.

00:31:44.290 --> 00:31:47.380
We've got people asking us
to include different charts.

00:31:47.380 --> 00:31:49.720
We've got people coming
to us with story ideas.

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:53.480
An enormous range of
information that both as--

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:57.220
both in terms of how we
iterate on this dataviz

00:31:57.220 --> 00:31:59.890
and how we just cover
the pandemic more broadly

00:31:59.890 --> 00:32:02.530
were incredibly,
incredibly valuable.

00:32:02.530 --> 00:32:04.990
And none of this information
would have come to us

00:32:04.990 --> 00:32:07.930
if we hadn't set up this inbox,
if we hadn't asked people

00:32:07.930 --> 00:32:10.077
to send these to us.

00:32:10.077 --> 00:32:11.910
So the lesson there, I
think, is very clear,

00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:18.150
which is that, if we want to
do the best that we can do,

00:32:18.150 --> 00:32:20.100
as data visualization
practitioners,

00:32:20.100 --> 00:32:23.130
both in terms of improving
the work that we do

00:32:23.130 --> 00:32:28.567
through feedback and in terms
of just reaching-- effectively

00:32:28.567 --> 00:32:30.150
reaching-- as many
people as possible,

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:33.540
and making them feel like
charts and dataviz are for them

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:37.080
and not just for data nerds,
continued communication

00:32:37.080 --> 00:32:38.830
is absolutely critical.

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:41.190
So I might suggest,
for example, that you

00:32:41.190 --> 00:32:44.790
might want to put a contact
email, or Twitter handle,

00:32:44.790 --> 00:32:47.170
or whatever works best
for you on your chart

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:49.710
sometimes so that wherever
someone encounters

00:32:49.710 --> 00:32:51.413
this piece of work
in the wild, they'll

00:32:51.413 --> 00:32:53.830
know they can come to you, and
they can give you thoughts.

00:32:53.830 --> 00:32:55.830
I just feel like
it's so invaluable.

00:32:55.830 --> 00:32:58.980
And you will learn so much
about what does and doesn't work

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:01.650
and the shades of gray
in-between by doing this.

00:33:01.650 --> 00:33:03.900
Whereas, I think there's
often a tendency for us,

00:33:03.900 --> 00:33:06.570
in dataviz, to see our job
as just making a thing.

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:09.070
And then we put the thing out
there, and we forget about it.

00:33:09.070 --> 00:33:10.770
But really, really,
if there's one thing

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:13.560
I can get to you from
today, it's, don't do that.

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:14.430
Stick around.

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:16.770
Enjoy the chat.

00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:20.520
The next point-- big, big
learning curve for me--

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:22.050
and again, that
audience feedback

00:33:22.050 --> 00:33:23.425
was absolutely
critical to this--

00:33:23.425 --> 00:33:27.600
was the risk of trying
to be too clever,

00:33:27.600 --> 00:33:32.400
trying to be too technical with
solutions to dataviz problems,

00:33:32.400 --> 00:33:34.860
and essentially losing
people along the way

00:33:34.860 --> 00:33:37.720
and losing trust along the way.

00:33:37.720 --> 00:33:40.570
So the term "blinding with
science," I'm sure most of you

00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:41.320
are familiar with.

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:42.945
But for those who
aren't, it's the idea

00:33:42.945 --> 00:33:45.150
that you're telling
someone about something

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:47.680
in an overly complex
or technical way

00:33:47.680 --> 00:33:51.600
so that regardless
of the result of what

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:54.330
you've done, they end up going
away scratching their head.

00:33:54.330 --> 00:33:55.770
They're not quite sure--

00:33:55.770 --> 00:33:59.083
because of the terminology,
because of the methodology,

00:33:59.083 --> 00:34:00.750
they're not quite
sure what you've done.

00:34:00.750 --> 00:34:03.600
And I think there's often a
risk with that in dataviz.

00:34:03.600 --> 00:34:06.750
So the question that we had--

00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:09.060
this was still back
in the spring--

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:14.460
was, could we improve the way
we drew the lines on our charts

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:17.400
so that instead of using
a simple seven-day rolling

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:21.630
average, as we had been doing,
we got slightly smoother lines?

00:34:21.630 --> 00:34:24.960
But we also got lines where the
final data point on the chart

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:28.199
was a more faithful
reflection of the final data

00:34:28.199 --> 00:34:31.020
point in real life, because, of
course, the problem when you're

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:33.335
doing a seven-day
average, whether you align

00:34:33.335 --> 00:34:35.460
that seven-day average to
the right, to the middle,

00:34:35.460 --> 00:34:37.530
or to the left, is,
it's never going

00:34:37.530 --> 00:34:41.370
to be an especially-close
representation of the last data

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:43.620
point you have,
because it's being

00:34:43.620 --> 00:34:45.840
influenced by the other six.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:48.300
And so the issue
we were having is,

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:51.635
the numbers in our headlines,
in our news stories at the FT,

00:34:51.635 --> 00:34:53.760
would always be referring
to that last data point--

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:55.409
the thing that
has just happened.

00:34:55.409 --> 00:34:58.360
But our charts were referring
to the seven-day average,

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:00.360
which was, while
cases were going up,

00:35:00.360 --> 00:35:01.505
always lower than that.

00:35:01.505 --> 00:35:02.880
And so we would
have this tension

00:35:02.880 --> 00:35:05.220
where the numbers on our
charts didn't quite reflect

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:06.940
the numbers in the news.

00:35:06.940 --> 00:35:09.600
So what we decided
to do was look

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:12.090
at whether we could
optimize the way we actually

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:14.490
draw the lines on the chart
so that they're still smooth,

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:17.640
as you get with an average,
but they also actually

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:20.738
more faithfully reflect the
very latest data point you have.

00:35:20.738 --> 00:35:22.530
So for those of you
who have only ever seen

00:35:22.530 --> 00:35:24.552
seven-day average
chart of COVID data,

00:35:24.552 --> 00:35:26.760
this is what the underlying
data actually looks like.

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:27.930
It's incredibly noisy.

00:35:27.930 --> 00:35:31.350
You get these huge
peaks and troughs.

00:35:31.350 --> 00:35:35.580
New daily cases will drop to
zero and then shoot up to 1,000

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:40.170
the next day, because the way
that this data is actually

00:35:40.170 --> 00:35:45.090
collected and reported leads
to enormous differences

00:35:45.090 --> 00:35:47.280
in the numbers you
get from day to day.

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:48.600
There's weekly cyclicality.

00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:50.200
There's all sorts
of stuff going on.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:52.870
So we definitely didn't want
the data to look like this.

00:35:52.870 --> 00:35:55.620
Now, of course, the
last point of each line

00:35:55.620 --> 00:35:58.290
here would reflect reality,
would reflect the last data

00:35:58.290 --> 00:35:59.280
point we had.

00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:00.420
But it's far too noisy.

00:36:00.420 --> 00:36:01.780
We couldn't publish that.

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:03.390
So here's the
rolling-average method

00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:05.520
we have been using-- much
clearer, much smoother.

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:07.020
You can see exactly
what's going on.

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:09.480
You get clear trends over time.

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:12.240
You can see here, for example,
that Italy had reached its peak

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:14.260
and started coming back down.

00:36:14.260 --> 00:36:16.413
But the last data point
on each of these lines

00:36:16.413 --> 00:36:18.330
didn't necessarily match
up with the last data

00:36:18.330 --> 00:36:20.420
point that had been recorded.

00:36:20.420 --> 00:36:23.800
So what we considered
was a smoothing spline.

00:36:23.800 --> 00:36:25.918
So it keeps that
smoothness of the lines.

00:36:25.918 --> 00:36:27.460
It's still relatively
easy to discern

00:36:27.460 --> 00:36:30.640
what's going on in one country
and compare it to another.

00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:33.753
But the last data
point is now, because

00:36:33.753 --> 00:36:35.170
of how the splines
are calculated,

00:36:35.170 --> 00:36:37.540
much closer to the last data
point that had been recorded.

00:36:37.540 --> 00:36:39.207
And you can see that
in this next chart.

00:36:39.207 --> 00:36:46.270
So we actually measured how
close to the final reported

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:49.180
data point was the last
data point of each line when

00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:50.230
we used this method.

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:52.360
On the left-hand side, you've
got a simple rolling average.

00:36:52.360 --> 00:36:54.310
On the right-hand side,
you've got the spline.

00:36:54.310 --> 00:36:55.960
And you can see
that the data points

00:36:55.960 --> 00:36:58.930
are much closer to the real
reported value on the right.

00:36:58.930 --> 00:37:02.140
So we decided, let's
switch over to the spline.

00:37:02.140 --> 00:37:05.470
And we did, again,
a long explainer

00:37:05.470 --> 00:37:08.710
of why we'd done
this, setting out

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:12.580
that need to better represent
the most recent data

00:37:12.580 --> 00:37:14.350
while keeping smoothness--

00:37:14.350 --> 00:37:17.380
big explanation out in the
public for anyone to see.

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:19.270
And I then, again,
followed my own advice--

00:37:19.270 --> 00:37:21.053
asked people, what
do they think?

00:37:21.053 --> 00:37:22.720
And here's the kind
of responses we got.

00:37:22.720 --> 00:37:26.130
And what you'll notice is
there were two quite clearly

00:37:26.130 --> 00:37:27.880
different camps here.

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:31.180
On the left-- top left-- we've
got a couple of people saying,

00:37:31.180 --> 00:37:31.690
splines--

00:37:31.690 --> 00:37:32.200
much better.

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:35.497
Yeah, I'm very devoted to
splining myself, for example.

00:37:35.497 --> 00:37:37.330
And the thing I should
point out immediately

00:37:37.330 --> 00:37:40.120
about that is that these two
people, Peter and Micah-- and I

00:37:40.120 --> 00:37:43.360
don't know if either of them are
actually watching along today--

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:46.850
are both data scientists
to some degree.

00:37:46.850 --> 00:37:50.620
So I think anyone
who's heard of splines

00:37:50.620 --> 00:37:53.380
felt very positive
about that as an idea.

00:37:53.380 --> 00:37:55.570
But then we get some
different feedback.

00:37:55.570 --> 00:37:57.483
In the upper right,
we've got someone

00:37:57.483 --> 00:37:58.900
saying the rolling
average is much

00:37:58.900 --> 00:38:01.370
easier for the average
person to understand.

00:38:01.370 --> 00:38:04.660
And in the lower left, we've
got someone with a data science

00:38:04.660 --> 00:38:07.360
background, but still saying
that the rolling average

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:09.110
probably makes more sense.

00:38:09.110 --> 00:38:11.560
And then in the lower
left, we actually

00:38:11.560 --> 00:38:13.780
have one of the epidemiologists
in the UK working

00:38:13.780 --> 00:38:18.370
on the development of the
vaccines talking about how

00:38:18.370 --> 00:38:21.160
this is for the general public.

00:38:21.160 --> 00:38:23.240
The audience for this
work is a mass audience.

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:26.620
It's an audience with a very
mixed visual and data literacy,

00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:29.110
and it's much easier for
people to get their head

00:38:29.110 --> 00:38:31.870
around a seven-day
average than a spline.

00:38:31.870 --> 00:38:36.340
So different people with
different personal contexts

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:38.900
had very different
views on this.

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:41.390
So the takeaway that we
had, again, from this

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:44.330
was that you have to prioritize
the ease of understanding.

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:46.910
You may have come up with
an incredibly technical,

00:38:46.910 --> 00:38:49.340
nifty solutions
that, on the basis

00:38:49.340 --> 00:38:53.282
of any sort of quantitative
tests, works better.

00:38:53.282 --> 00:38:55.490
But if that is going to
confuse people along the way,

00:38:55.490 --> 00:38:56.580
that is a problem.

00:38:56.580 --> 00:38:58.670
And again, for me,
the fundamental goal

00:38:58.670 --> 00:39:00.950
of my data visualizations
is communication

00:39:00.950 --> 00:39:02.600
with a mass audience.

00:39:02.600 --> 00:39:07.130
And I would rather have
clear communication,

00:39:07.130 --> 00:39:09.800
as many people as
possible understanding,

00:39:09.800 --> 00:39:12.380
remembering, and wanting to
share these charts than to have

00:39:12.380 --> 00:39:16.700
something which is
5% more correct,

00:39:16.700 --> 00:39:20.738
shall we say, but loses a
lot of people along the way.

00:39:20.738 --> 00:39:22.280
The final lesson
that we've learned--

00:39:22.280 --> 00:39:25.010
and just in the last couple
of weeks, I think, in our work

00:39:25.010 --> 00:39:29.810
on the pandemic-- has been the
importance and just the way

00:39:29.810 --> 00:39:34.160
that you can use animation
to tell a story much, much

00:39:34.160 --> 00:39:36.650
more effectively than
telling that same story

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:38.930
in a static chart.

00:39:38.930 --> 00:39:41.462
And so I've done a
couple bits of animation

00:39:41.462 --> 00:39:42.920
over the last couple
of years which

00:39:42.920 --> 00:39:44.240
have been especially effective.

00:39:44.240 --> 00:39:46.407
And I think the lesson I've
really learned from this

00:39:46.407 --> 00:39:51.650
is that the sense of tension
and of anticipation that you can

00:39:51.650 --> 00:39:55.580
build up when you
do animation well

00:39:55.580 --> 00:39:59.270
can produce a much, much
stronger response from people,

00:39:59.270 --> 00:40:02.180
a much stronger reaction, than
doing exactly the same chart

00:40:02.180 --> 00:40:03.770
as a static chart.

00:40:03.770 --> 00:40:06.025
So I'm going to show you
two examples of that.

00:40:06.025 --> 00:40:07.400
They're examples
that some of you

00:40:07.400 --> 00:40:10.640
may have seen in
recent days, which

00:40:10.640 --> 00:40:14.570
are where I wanted to make
the point that COVID really

00:40:14.570 --> 00:40:18.320
is considerably worse, for
example, than a bad flu.

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:19.940
So here's the first chart.

00:40:19.940 --> 00:40:21.580
And what we're looking at here--

00:40:21.580 --> 00:40:22.460
again, this is a GIF.

00:40:22.460 --> 00:40:23.060
It's an animation.

00:40:23.060 --> 00:40:23.830
It's going to loop.

00:40:23.830 --> 00:40:24.780
So if you miss it
the first time,

00:40:24.780 --> 00:40:26.180
you'll see it the second time.

00:40:26.180 --> 00:40:29.495
We're looking at the weekly rate
of people admitted into ICU--

00:40:29.495 --> 00:40:31.670
so Intensive Care Units--

00:40:31.670 --> 00:40:36.152
in England with flu in a typical
winter over the last few years,

00:40:36.152 --> 00:40:37.610
and then contrasting
that with what

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:40.340
we've seen this year for COVID.

00:40:40.340 --> 00:40:44.000
And you'll see, quite clearly,
that flu admissions in the past

00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:44.540
were--

00:40:44.540 --> 00:40:46.160
even when we thought
they were high--

00:40:46.160 --> 00:40:47.743
so even when we had
what, at the time,

00:40:47.743 --> 00:40:51.140
was seen as record-bad
flu winters,

00:40:51.140 --> 00:40:53.870
those figures were
much, much lower

00:40:53.870 --> 00:40:56.480
than what we've seen over
the last month or two

00:40:56.480 --> 00:40:58.610
in England with COVID.

00:40:58.610 --> 00:41:02.150
So this was really attempt
to put to bed this myth--

00:41:02.150 --> 00:41:05.120
this idea-- that,
oh, COVID is just--

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:06.320
it's just flu rebranded.

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:07.820
It's just a new
version of what we'd

00:41:07.820 --> 00:41:09.695
seen before-- and to
show that this really is

00:41:09.695 --> 00:41:12.003
a completely different beast.

00:41:12.003 --> 00:41:13.670
Now, some people would
come back to this

00:41:13.670 --> 00:41:16.250
and say, well, we're just
testing a lot more for COVID

00:41:16.250 --> 00:41:19.260
this year, so that's
why we see the numbers--

00:41:19.260 --> 00:41:21.960
we see much higher numbers
than we usually would with flu.

00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:23.810
But again, I had another
animated response

00:41:23.810 --> 00:41:26.420
to that, which is to show
the total numbers of people

00:41:26.420 --> 00:41:27.140
in ICU--

00:41:27.140 --> 00:41:29.330
in intensive care-- in London--

00:41:29.330 --> 00:41:32.840
London, UK-- for any reason,
and compare that, again,

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:34.520
from winter to winter.

00:41:34.520 --> 00:41:38.660
So in a typical year, even
during a bad flu season,

00:41:38.660 --> 00:41:41.480
the numbers of people in
intensive care in London

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:43.760
never go above about 750.

00:41:43.760 --> 00:41:46.370
So again, this is whether
they're in there for flu,

00:41:46.370 --> 00:41:49.400
whether they're in there for
a heart attack, for a stroke--

00:41:49.400 --> 00:41:50.340
any of that.

00:41:50.340 --> 00:41:52.700
But the numbers of
people in there for all

00:41:52.700 --> 00:41:54.920
causes never really
go above about 750,

00:41:54.920 --> 00:41:57.620
and are typically around 700,
whereas this year, they've

00:41:57.620 --> 00:42:00.680
gone up above 1,200, and
in fact, in recent days,

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:02.720
have gone closer to
1,400-- so almost

00:42:02.720 --> 00:42:05.300
double the number of people
in intensive care in London

00:42:05.300 --> 00:42:06.770
this year than a usual year.

00:42:06.770 --> 00:42:10.130
And unless there's been a sudden
surge in the numbers of people

00:42:10.130 --> 00:42:12.643
falling down multiple
flights of stairs,

00:42:12.643 --> 00:42:14.060
we can point to
the one thing that

00:42:14.060 --> 00:42:16.190
is different about this
year, which is COVID.

00:42:16.190 --> 00:42:19.070
But the point is,
for what I consider

00:42:19.070 --> 00:42:23.120
to be a really important topic
here, which is correcting

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:26.747
relatively widely-held myths
that what we were seeing

00:42:26.747 --> 00:42:28.580
this winter was nothing
out of the ordinary,

00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:30.763
and therefore, we don't
need to be in lockdown,

00:42:30.763 --> 00:42:32.930
we don't need any restrictions,
because this is just

00:42:32.930 --> 00:42:36.800
like any other winter, you
gain an enormous amount

00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:39.230
by producing this
as an animation.

00:42:39.230 --> 00:42:42.920
It's a much more
engaging piece of work.

00:42:42.920 --> 00:42:45.680
And this graphic
on Twitter alone

00:42:45.680 --> 00:42:49.580
has now been seen by more
than 10 million people, which

00:42:49.580 --> 00:42:50.390
is far--

00:42:50.390 --> 00:42:53.450
more people have seen this
graphic than any other chart

00:42:53.450 --> 00:42:57.290
that we produce at the FT
all year on social media.

00:42:57.290 --> 00:42:58.473
And that, I think--

00:42:58.473 --> 00:43:00.140
and in fact, it's not
just what I think.

00:43:00.140 --> 00:43:01.820
A lot of the responses
on social media

00:43:01.820 --> 00:43:03.770
have explicitly pointed to this.

00:43:03.770 --> 00:43:06.770
The use of animation
was enormously--

00:43:06.770 --> 00:43:09.620
was pivotal to that in terms
of how it grabs attention

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:12.110
more than a static chart,
in terms of how it builds

00:43:12.110 --> 00:43:14.480
suspense, and in terms of
how you get that shock--

00:43:14.480 --> 00:43:17.750
that surprise factor at the
end-- when you compare the two.

00:43:17.750 --> 00:43:21.820
So the key point there being,
where it's suitable, animation

00:43:21.820 --> 00:43:23.440
is an incredibly
powerful technique

00:43:23.440 --> 00:43:25.070
to use to convey a message.

00:43:25.070 --> 00:43:26.890
Now, I think it should
be used sparingly.

00:43:26.890 --> 00:43:30.390
If you suddenly make everything
animated, it becomes--

00:43:30.390 --> 00:43:32.860
it's like the old
days of '90s internet

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:35.070
where you've got
pop-ups everywhere.

00:43:35.070 --> 00:43:36.070
Everything is too noisy.

00:43:36.070 --> 00:43:37.660
It's hard to actually get
across your key point,

00:43:37.660 --> 00:43:39.095
and everything gets drowned out.

00:43:39.095 --> 00:43:40.720
But using animation
sparingly, I think,

00:43:40.720 --> 00:43:44.040
is an incredibly
powerful technique.

00:43:44.040 --> 00:43:48.080
So all of that is to say that
the last 12 months, I think,

00:43:48.080 --> 00:43:50.580
have been an incredible crash
course for me and for our team

00:43:50.580 --> 00:43:53.010
at the FT in terms
of really getting

00:43:53.010 --> 00:43:54.510
to the fundamentals
of how people

00:43:54.510 --> 00:43:58.005
do read charts, and therefore,
what we should be thinking

00:43:58.005 --> 00:44:00.380
about when we make charts--
what we should be focusing on

00:44:00.380 --> 00:44:02.040
and prioritizing.

00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:06.660
And coming into this year,
I think my assumption--

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:08.630
and a lot of people
here, I'm sure--

00:44:08.630 --> 00:44:11.130
the assumption would have been
that people read charts a bit

00:44:11.130 --> 00:44:11.630
like this.

00:44:11.630 --> 00:44:15.120
They get out their protractor,
their set square, their ruler,

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:16.350
and their compass.

00:44:16.350 --> 00:44:19.620
And it's all about precision,
and people are really

00:44:19.620 --> 00:44:24.120
focusing on individual pixels
and where different elements

00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:25.620
are in your plot
when they're trying

00:44:25.620 --> 00:44:27.310
to take away that message.

00:44:27.310 --> 00:44:29.910
They're almost trying to
parse the data set back out

00:44:29.910 --> 00:44:30.540
of your chart.

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:32.970
And so you, as the
practitioner, should really

00:44:32.970 --> 00:44:36.480
be focusing on the most
high-fidelity, most clear

00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:40.530
encoding of that
data as possible.

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:43.530
But the reality, I think, is
actually a bit more like this.

00:44:43.530 --> 00:44:45.990
It's a bit more like people
going to a movie theater

00:44:45.990 --> 00:44:48.060
and sitting back and watching.

00:44:48.060 --> 00:44:52.800
They are coming to a
chart to see something,

00:44:52.800 --> 00:44:55.350
to see some kind of story,
to see-- to get a message,

00:44:55.350 --> 00:44:58.995
to take away something that is
playing out in front of them--

00:44:58.995 --> 00:45:00.870
in some cases, literally,
with the animation,

00:45:00.870 --> 00:45:03.030
but in other cases,
it's a line chart.

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:04.680
But it's a line chart
where they're not

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:08.698
focusing on tiny differences
between data points

00:45:08.698 --> 00:45:09.240
on the chart.

00:45:09.240 --> 00:45:11.400
They're focusing on, what
is this top-line message

00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:12.070
in the title?

00:45:12.070 --> 00:45:13.290
What's the takeaway here?

00:45:13.290 --> 00:45:15.390
And what are the
other sweeping trends

00:45:15.390 --> 00:45:17.070
that this chart is conveying?

00:45:17.070 --> 00:45:18.690
They're not poring
over it trying

00:45:18.690 --> 00:45:23.050
to extract the values from
every pixel on the page.

00:45:23.050 --> 00:45:25.890
So to summarize, the key lessons
I think I've learned here--

00:45:25.890 --> 00:45:28.223
and I think these are lessons
that I'm going to be using

00:45:28.223 --> 00:45:30.190
for the rest of my career--

00:45:30.190 --> 00:45:32.460
first of all, text is critical.

00:45:32.460 --> 00:45:34.950
I know we're all in
the dataviz industry.

00:45:34.950 --> 00:45:36.160
We make charts.

00:45:36.160 --> 00:45:38.700
But if we don't get the
text right, none of the rest

00:45:38.700 --> 00:45:39.570
really matters.

00:45:39.570 --> 00:45:40.410
People will be lost.

00:45:40.410 --> 00:45:41.850
They'll be confused.

00:45:41.850 --> 00:45:43.540
It will all be a disaster.

00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:46.110
The second point-- we have
to take responsibility.

00:45:46.110 --> 00:45:49.080
If someone is confused about
our chart, that's our fault.

00:45:49.080 --> 00:45:50.880
And the best thing we
can do to combat that

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:55.673
is to include explanations
in situ around where

00:45:55.673 --> 00:45:58.215
that chart appears to make sure
that there's as little as can

00:45:58.215 --> 00:45:59.400
be possible confusion.

00:45:59.400 --> 00:46:02.550
On a similar note, we
need to be considering

00:46:02.550 --> 00:46:05.160
what are people's beliefs,
feelings, emotions,

00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:06.990
sentiments around
this subject matter,

00:46:06.990 --> 00:46:09.570
and how could they color
their reaction to this chart?

00:46:09.570 --> 00:46:12.360
How can we design this chart in
as neutral a way as possible,

00:46:12.360 --> 00:46:15.528
in that sense, so that
at least if anyone

00:46:15.528 --> 00:46:17.070
does have any issues
with that chart,

00:46:17.070 --> 00:46:18.653
it's because they
don't like the data?

00:46:18.653 --> 00:46:23.880
It's not because they feel
put off or hurt somehow

00:46:23.880 --> 00:46:26.500
by the way we've actually
conveyed the information.

00:46:26.500 --> 00:46:27.970
The next step--
and I think, again,

00:46:27.970 --> 00:46:30.600
one of the most important--
don't just publish and vanish.

00:46:30.600 --> 00:46:31.260
Stick around.

00:46:31.260 --> 00:46:32.635
Get involved in
the conversation.

00:46:32.635 --> 00:46:35.325
You will learn so much about
how people are understanding

00:46:35.325 --> 00:46:37.590
your chart that
you can incorporate

00:46:37.590 --> 00:46:39.420
into the next one you make.

00:46:39.420 --> 00:46:42.480
And make sure that your
chart, above all else,

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:43.900
is easy to understand.

00:46:43.900 --> 00:46:46.410
It doesn't matter
if you can fine-tune

00:46:46.410 --> 00:46:51.970
it to make it 2%, 3%, 4%
more faithful to the data.

00:46:51.970 --> 00:46:54.960
It doesn't even matter, I think,
as much as-- it doesn't matter

00:46:54.960 --> 00:46:58.380
as much as we think it
does about optimizing

00:46:58.380 --> 00:47:02.700
tiny little things about
how people understand

00:47:02.700 --> 00:47:05.310
uncertainty, or ranges,
or confidence intervals,

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:06.650
for example.

00:47:06.650 --> 00:47:08.400
The key thing is that
you focus on getting

00:47:08.400 --> 00:47:11.580
across that core message and
getting it across accessibly.

00:47:11.580 --> 00:47:15.240
And finally, we
should all be aware

00:47:15.240 --> 00:47:18.180
that we have this incredibly
powerful tool with animation.

00:47:18.180 --> 00:47:21.210
And there are plenty of charts
where we would unthinkingly

00:47:21.210 --> 00:47:22.740
just produce a static chart.

00:47:22.740 --> 00:47:25.140
But an animation can
have that much more--

00:47:25.140 --> 00:47:29.070
I would say, in fact, orders
of magnitude more impact.

00:47:29.070 --> 00:47:32.160
And I'm going to end with a call
to action in terms of things

00:47:32.160 --> 00:47:34.380
that I would like us, as
the dataviz community,

00:47:34.380 --> 00:47:38.490
to do differently, or do more,
or even do for the first time

00:47:38.490 --> 00:47:42.990
as we go away from this, which
is that, first of all, as I

00:47:42.990 --> 00:47:44.490
mentioned at the
very start, there's

00:47:44.490 --> 00:47:47.100
a huge amount of
resources out there

00:47:47.100 --> 00:47:50.630
on how to achieve particular
technical or visual effects

00:47:50.630 --> 00:47:51.190
in a chart.

00:47:51.190 --> 00:47:54.030
But there's not very
much on the general way

00:47:54.030 --> 00:47:57.300
of framing your work and the
language you use around it

00:47:57.300 --> 00:47:59.820
in order to achieve the
clearest-possible communication

00:47:59.820 --> 00:48:00.490
of a message.

00:48:00.490 --> 00:48:01.865
So what I'd love
to see, and what

00:48:01.865 --> 00:48:04.530
I hope to participate in
myself, is putting out

00:48:04.530 --> 00:48:09.930
more resources that can help
people think of and refine

00:48:09.930 --> 00:48:13.020
this idea of dataviz as
communication more than dataviz

00:48:13.020 --> 00:48:16.500
just as a technical
exercise of creating

00:48:16.500 --> 00:48:20.400
the most objectively-good
chart possible.

00:48:20.400 --> 00:48:23.130
And secondly, I'd
like us to be more--

00:48:23.130 --> 00:48:26.010
to think about accessibility
in the broadest of senses

00:48:26.010 --> 00:48:27.810
when we make our charts.

00:48:27.810 --> 00:48:30.720
Because we love making
charts, and because we

00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:32.490
know other people--

00:48:32.490 --> 00:48:37.230
most people on this call,
I'm sure-- on this session--

00:48:37.230 --> 00:48:39.810
love looking at
charts, it can be

00:48:39.810 --> 00:48:43.020
very easy to get into
the trap of thinking,

00:48:43.020 --> 00:48:46.120
oh, what's the coolest thing
I can do here that other chart

00:48:46.120 --> 00:48:47.160
people will love?

00:48:47.160 --> 00:48:48.480
How can I push the envelope?

00:48:48.480 --> 00:48:53.640
How can I make this
chart especially exciting

00:48:53.640 --> 00:48:56.790
and impressive to
people who like charts?

00:48:56.790 --> 00:49:00.420
But that is the tiniest
tip of the iceberg.

00:49:00.420 --> 00:49:03.660
And the rest of this enormous
thing beneath the surface

00:49:03.660 --> 00:49:05.640
of the sea is non-chart people.

00:49:05.640 --> 00:49:08.310
It's people who last
looked at charts when

00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:10.640
they were age 16, 17, 18,
and then got the hell away

00:49:10.640 --> 00:49:11.140
from them.

00:49:11.140 --> 00:49:14.400
They're probably people who
try to even avoid spreadsheets.

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:18.330
But that is an enormous audience
of people who could love charts

00:49:18.330 --> 00:49:20.400
if they just had charts
that spoke to that,

00:49:20.400 --> 00:49:23.430
and charts that didn't
try to be too smart,

00:49:23.430 --> 00:49:25.093
charts that didn't
condescend to them.

00:49:25.093 --> 00:49:26.760
So the other thing
I'd love us all to do

00:49:26.760 --> 00:49:28.830
is think more
about making charts

00:49:28.830 --> 00:49:31.230
not that are graphics
for graphics editors,

00:49:31.230 --> 00:49:34.080
as someone once said
to me, but charts that

00:49:34.080 --> 00:49:38.280
are for your mom, your
dad, and everyone else

00:49:38.280 --> 00:49:40.753
out there in the world.

00:49:40.753 --> 00:49:42.170
So thank you very
much for bearing

00:49:42.170 --> 00:49:43.500
with me through all of this.

00:49:43.500 --> 00:49:47.137
And please do fire me
all your questions.

00:49:47.137 --> 00:49:49.220
And I'd also encourage
you-- in the spirit of what

00:49:49.220 --> 00:49:51.773
I've been saying here, do
send me a tweet, or an email,

00:49:51.773 --> 00:49:53.940
or anything like that with
other questions you have,

00:49:53.940 --> 00:49:55.340
if you don't get time for
them just now, because I'd

00:49:55.340 --> 00:49:57.650
love to hear your thoughts
on all of this stuff.

00:50:05.150 --> 00:50:07.900
[MUSICAL CHORD]

